# =============================================================================
# Docker Build Context Exclusions - Repository Root
# =============================================================================
# Docker looks for an ignore file in TWO places, and the first one it finds
# wins outright. A file named after the Dockerfile passed to `-f` - that is
# <dockerfile-path>.dockerignore, which here would be
# infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile.dockerignore - takes PRECEDENCE. Only when
# no such file exists does Docker fall back to <context>/.dockerignore, which
# is this file. Precedence means REPLACEMENT rather than merge: adding a
# Dockerfile-specific ignore file would switch off every rule below at once,
# and no build would report that it had happened. None exists in this
# repository, which is why this file is the one in force - and why none may
# ever be created beside infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile.
#
# Both build paths in this repository set the context to the repository root:
#
#   .github/workflows/cd.yml            context: .       -> repository root
#   infrastructure/docker/docker-compose.yml
#                                       context: ../..   -> repository root
#
# So this file is what actually filters every build here. The older
# infrastructure/docker/.dockerignore sits BELOW both context roots, is never
# loaded by any build path, and is kept only as a record of intent.
#
# HOW THESE PATTERNS MATCH: paths are matched RELATIVE TO THE CONTEXT ROOT,
# which is the repository root - not relative to the Dockerfile's directory.
# A bare pattern such as `node_modules/` therefore matches only a top-level
# directory and would NOT match src/backend/node_modules. The backend artifacts
# excluded below - dependencies, coverage output, the development tooling
# configuration and the environment files - all live one or two levels down
# inside src/backend/, so for those the recursive `**/` form is required rather
# than optional. The entries that sit AT the context root (.git, docs/ and
# infrastructure/) are deliberately written bare, because a bare pattern is
# exactly what matches a top-level path; and src/backend/tests is spelled out
# in full because it is the only tests directory in the repository.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED, because the build reads them from the context:
#   src/backend/package.json, src/backend/package-lock.json  (dependency install)
#   src/backend/{server,app}.js and config/ middleware/ routes/ utils/  (sources)
#
# The Dockerfile named with `-f` and this ignore file are always sent to the
# daemon regardless of the rules below, so excluding infrastructure/ is safe.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependency directories
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the most important exclusion in the file. Both image stages read the
# application sources straight from the build context - `COPY src/backend/ ./`
# at Dockerfile:30 in the builder stage and again at Dockerfile:62 in the
# runtime stage - so a developer's local src/backend/node_modules would not
# travel from one stage to the other; the context copy would place it directly
# into each of them.
#
# The harm is done by the runtime-stage copy. It runs AFTER
# `npm ci --omit=dev --only=production` (Dockerfile:57) has installed the
# production-only dependency tree into that same directory, so a copied
# development node_modules lands on top of the production install and replaces
# it - shipping an image that is larger and carries the test and tooling
# packages it must never contain. The recursive form prevents that at the
# source, by keeping the directory out of the context in the first place.
**/node_modules

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test coverage output and log files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `**/coverage` is generated locally by `npm test`, which collects coverage on
# every run.
#
# `**/*.log` is not written by this application: src/backend/utils/logger.js
# emits through console.log to stdout and console.error to stderr, and no
# module under src/backend opens a file, so the running process creates no log
# file of its own. The pattern covers the log files that collect around it
# instead - output a developer has redirected into a file, a stream captured by
# a process manager or container runtime, and tooling logs such as
# npm-debug.log. Local artifacts either way, never build input.
**/coverage
**/*.log

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development tooling configuration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test runner, file watcher and code-style configuration are development-only.
# The production image runs `npm start` and reads none of them, so shipping
# them would only widen the image and its attack surface.
**/jest.config.js
**/nodemon.json
**/.eslintrc.js
**/.prettierrc

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment files, registry credentials and keys
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local configuration must never be baked into an image; the container is
# configured through environment variables at run time instead. The source
# copies (Dockerfile:30 and :62) take whatever the context holds, so anything
# left unexcluded here is committed to an image layer and travels with it.
#
# Exact filenames are not enough. `**/.env` matches the literal name `.env`
# only: it does not match `.env.example`, and it does not match the local and
# per-environment variants either - `.env.local`, `.env.development.local`,
# `.env.test.local`, `.env.production.local`, `.env.test`, `.env.production`.
# `**/.env.*` covers all of them, whatever the suffix. `**/.env.example` is
# kept as its own line even though `**/.env.*` also matches it, because
# excluding the template is a deliberate decision rather than a side effect:
# the running image never reads it - src/backend/config/index.js loads `.env`
# via dotenv - and it is a learner artifact with no runtime role.
#
# `**/.npmrc` keeps registry credentials and auth tokens out of the context (no
# .npmrc exists in this repository, and the image's `npm ci` needs none), and
# the key and certificate patterns mirror the sensitive set the root .gitignore
# already refuses to track (.gitignore:137-140). All of these are untracked
# local files when they exist at all, which is exactly why Git's rules cannot
# protect the build context and this file has to.
**/.env
**/.env.example
**/.env.*
**/.npmrc
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/*.csr

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The suites run locally and in CI against the source tree, not inside the
# production image. Path-qualified because this is the only tests directory.
src/backend/tests

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository history is large, changes on every commit (needlessly
# invalidating build cache) and has no runtime purpose.
.git

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation and infrastructure definitions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tutorial documentation and the container, Compose and Kubernetes definitions
# describe how to build and deploy the application; the application itself does
# not read them.
docs/
infrastructure/
